### SSML Prosody Element Examples

Source: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/speech-service/speech-synthesis-markup-voice

Demonstrates various ways to use the SSML 'prosody' element to adjust speech characteristics. This includes setting pitch, contour, range, rate, and volume using absolute, relative, and enumerated values. Note that pitch contour is best applied to longer phrases or sentences.

```xml
<speak version="1.0" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2001/10/synthesis" xml:lang="en-US">
  <prosody pitch="600Hz">This is text with an absolute pitch.</prosody>
  <prosody pitch="+80Hz">This is text with a relative pitch increase.</prosody>
  <prosody pitch="-2st">This is text with a relative pitch decrease in semitones.</prosody>
  <prosody pitch="50%">This is text with a relative pitch change of 50%.</prosody>
  <prosody pitch="x-high">This is text with a very high pitch.</prosody>
  <prosody contour="(0%,+20Hz) (10%,-2st) (40%,+10Hz)">This text has a pitch contour applied.</prosody>
  <prosody range="10st">This text has a pitch range of 10 semitones.</prosody>
  <prosody rate="0.5">This text is spoken at half the normal rate.</prosody>
  <prosody rate="fast">This text is spoken at a fast rate.</prosody>
  <prosody volume="silent">This text will be silent.</prosody>
  <prosody volume="loud">This text will be spoken loudly.</prosody>
  <prosody volume="+3%">This text has a 3% volume increase.</prosody>
</speak>
```

